<!-- house:begin: rendered from config/house.php by composer update; this project's rules go after house:end and outrank it -->
# CLAUDE.md — app/Requests (FormRequests)

One endpoint ⇒ one FormRequest carrying ALL of its rules. No inline `$request->validate([...])` or
`Validator::make` in a controller, and no `app/Http/Requests/` (`app/CLAUDE.md`, Layout).

- `authorize()` returns the authentication check and nothing more. Resource authorization is the policy's.
- **Server-owned columns get `['missing']`**: owner ids such as `user_id` (the owner is `$request->user()`, never
  the body), `status`, anything the server computes. It pairs with the model's non-fillable column. Never
  `prohibited`: it passes `null`, `''` and `[]`, which `validated()` then carries into `fill()`.
- Enum fields validate with `Rule::enum(X::class)`, never a hand-typed `in:` list that can drift.
- An integer in a JSON body is `integer:strict`: plain `integer` accepts `1500.0` and `1e3`. Form posts and query
  strings arrive as strings, which `strict` refuses, so there it stays `integer`. Money is an integer in minor units.
<!-- if:tenancy -->
- Every `exists` or `unique` rule on a tenant-owned table scopes to the tenant in a closure:
  `Rule::exists('projects', 'id')->where(fn ($q) => $q->where('tenant_id', CurrentTenant::id()))`, the same on
  `Rule::unique`. Never the eager `->where('tenant_id', CurrentTenant::id())`: it runs when `rules()` is built, and
  `CurrentTenant::id()` throws without a tenant. The database half is the composite foreign key
  (`database/CLAUDE.md`).
<!-- endif -->
- Free text is validated for **size and shape only**, never content-filtered (`app/CLAUDE.md`). Bound everything a
  user can post: `max:` on every string and array, size + mime on uploads.
- A FormRequest never decides the response shape.
<!-- if:htmx -->
  htmx callers get the same 422; the controller re-renders the form fragment with `$errors`.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- if:spa -->

## Exported forms

**Every FormRequest a SvelteKit form posts to is exported** with `#[ExportValidation('<kebab-name>')]`
(`PetarSpasic\LaravelHouse\Validation\ExportValidation`, the name unique in the app). `php artisan validation:export`
writes its Zod module into `frontend/src/lib/validation/generated/`; commit it with the FormRequest or `lang/` change,
or `npm run check` fails. Upload fields stay server-only.

- **Fortify's forms too.** Every Fortify form a SvelteKit page posts is backed by an exported FormRequest in
  `app/Requests/Auth/`.
  - The `app/Actions/Fortify/` actions validate with it: `$form = new RegisterRequest()`, then
    `Validator::make($input, $form->rules(), $form->messages(), $form->attributes())->validate()`. Never
    `app(RegisterRequest::class)` or injection: resolving a FormRequest validates the live request on the spot.
  - Where Fortify validates in its own `Laravel\Fortify\Http\Requests\*` class, the exported one extends that class
    and is bound over it in `FortifyServiceProvider`.
  - Where Fortify validates inline, the exported rules are never stricter than Fortify's.
- **`rules()` reads nothing eagerly.** The export runs it with `$this->route()` and `$this->user()` null. Read them
  null-safely or in a closure, only into server-only rules: `->ignore($this->route('project'))`,
  `->ignore($this->user()?->id)`, `->where(fn ($q) => …)`. Never into a browser rule
  (`'max:'.($this->user()?->limit ?? 10)` would export the fallback). A throw refuses the form.
<!-- if:tenancy -->
  No tenant is set at export either: the closure form above stays server-only; the eager form refuses the form.
<!-- endif -->
- **No `prepareForValidation()`**: the browser would check the unprepared input and could refuse what the server
  accepts.
- **Every string or number field** (`*` elements included) carries `required`, `nullable`, `filled`, `accepted` or
  `declined`. `sometimes` or a conditional `required_*` alone is not enough, because `''` reaches the rules as null.
- **Without `nullable`, `required` comes first.** Laravel runs `string|required` on the null that `''` became and
  reports both messages.
- **Every input the backend reads** (e.g. `remember`) is declared with a type: the schema strips undeclared keys.
- Every `*` field has a name in `attributes()`.
- **Arrays are never nullable in the schema**: an empty one posts `[]`. A list that needs items is
  `required|array|min:N`; an optional one carries no lower bound (the export refuses one).
- **An array keyed by strings is a map:** name it in `maps:` on the attribute, or the export takes it for a list. Maps
  and arrays of arrays make the form's `dataType` `'json'` (`references/validation-export.md`).
- **A `regex` with the `i` modifier also needs `u`. Never the `m` modifier.** The export refuses both.
- A refusal is fixed in the rule, or the check moves into a rule object, which stays server-only. Never drop the
  attribute to silence it.
- Each exported form has its parity proof (`tests/CLAUDE.md`); `npm run check` fails without it. The command, the
  mapping and the gate:
  `laravel-project-setup`'s `references/validation-export.md`.
<!-- endif -->
<!-- house:end -->
